stride 68 floats) and issues ONE 16B global store per thread to NHWC, so both global directions become 128-bit.\n5. Guard in fused_block: use vectorized kernels only when (HW % 4 == 0) && C == 256 && all data_ptrs are 16B aligned (true for at::empty); otherwise launch the existing scalar kernels verbatim, keeping 2x256x64x64 and any odd shape safe.\n6. Unchanged: launch counts, both cuDNN conv calls, weight packing, gn_finalize, stream usage, allocations, NCHW output format.\n7. Grid sizing must still give >= 2 waves/SM (mid shape: stats 2048 blocks, apply 4096, transpose/epilogue 4096 -> ok). Compile with -Xptxas -v; keep registers <= 48/thread, and if the apply kernel spills reduce the unroll from 8 to 4.\n8. Numerics: identical fp32 FMA/SiLU expressions; only stats summation order changes (still hierarchical fp32), well inside max_atol 0.0028.",
  "evidence": "gn_apply_silu_res_nchw_kernel: 114.75us, dram 88.81%, sm 19.41%, long_scoreboard 68.77%. gn_apply_silu_kernel: 74.9us, long_scoreboard 91.96%, sm 12.85%, dram 85.66%. gn_stats_kernel moves 67.1MB in 45.8us = 1.465 TB/s versus the ~1.765 TB/s peak implied by NCU (1.538 TB/s at 87.14%), i.e. a 13-18% efficiency gap despite 85-100% achieved occupancy. Root cause: purely scalar 32-bit accesses give too few outstanding bytes per warp, so DRAM latency (not bandwidth or warp count) caps these streaming kernels. Refuting the alternative 'conv is the bottleneck': the two cuDNN TF32 convs already run at 96.6 TFLOPS = 92% of the 104.8 TFLOPS roofline and the algorithm-switch attempt (#3) regressed to 1.1042; refuting 'occupancy-bound': achieved occupancy is 89.8-99.4% with 0 divergent branches, so adding warps cannot help.",
